      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nEXISTS checks whether the subquery returns at least one matching row and is especially natural for correlated existence tests. IN compares a value against the set returned by a subquery. Modern MySQL can optimize many equivalent forms, so neither is universally faster. A major correctness issue is NULL behavior with NOT IN: if the subquery can produce NULL, NOT IN can yield UNKNOWN and unexpectedly return no rows. NOT EXISTS is often safer for anti-joins.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.id\nFROM customers c\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1\n  FROM orders o\n  WHERE o.customer_id = c.id\n);\n```\n\nThis returns customers having at least one order.",
