      sampleAnswer: "First define the duplicate key and the row that should survive. Back up or test the operation, preferably inside an appropriate transaction, and verify the rows to be deleted with a SELECT before running DELETE. A common MySQL pattern is to use ROW_NUMBER() to assign one row to keep and delete the others, or use a self-join when a stable unique ID exists. After cleanup, add a UNIQUE constraint when business rules require uniqueness so the problem cannot recur.",
      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFirst define the duplicate key and the row that should survive. Back up or test the operation, preferably inside an appropriate transaction, and verify the rows to be deleted with a SELECT before running DELETE. A common MySQL pattern is to use ROW_NUMBER() to assign one row to keep and delete the others, or use a self-join when a stable unique ID exists. After cleanup, add a UNIQUE constraint when business rules require uniqueness so the problem cannot recur.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT id,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn\n  FROM customers\n)\nDELETE c\nFROM customers c\nJOIN ranked r ON r.id = c.id\nWHERE r.rn > 1;\n```\n\nTest the corresponding SELECT first and adapt the exact DELETE syntax to the MySQL version and transaction strategy.",
