      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA covering index contains all columns required by a query, so the database can obtain the needed result from the index without fetching the full table row. This can reduce I/O, especially for large tables. Whether a query is actually covered depends on the selected columns and the optimizer's chosen access path. Covering indexes increase index size, so they should be justified by important query patterns.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_date_amount\nON orders(customer_id, order_date, amount);\n```\n\nA query selecting customer_id, order_date, and amount with matching predicates may be satisfied entirely from the index.",
