      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nACID describes key transaction properties. Atomicity means a transaction's changes are treated as a unit. Consistency means committed work preserves defined database constraints and invariants. Isolation controls how concurrent transactions interact and what intermediate states they can observe. Durability means committed changes survive failures according to the database's durability mechanisms. The exact behavior depends on the storage engine, isolation level, configuration, and failure model.\n\n**Example:**\n\nIn a bank transfer, atomicity prevents only the debit from being committed if the credit fails; durability means a successfully committed transfer is expected to survive a server restart.",
