      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nReplication lag is the delay between a change being committed on the source and becoming visible or fully applied on a replica. Causes can include heavy write volume, slow replica I/O, long-running transactions, network delays, or insufficient replica resources. Lag can produce stale reads and complicate failover. Monitoring should track replication health and workload, not only a single lag number.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA payment is committed on the primary at 10:00:00, but a replica applies the change at 10:00:03.\n\nA read routed to that replica during the interval may not see the payment.",
