      detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA trigger is database logic that automatically executes in response to events such as INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE. Triggers can enforce certain auditing or derived-data rules close to the data, but they also create implicit side effects: application developers may not realize extra work occurs, debugging becomes harder, and bulk operations can become expensive. Triggers should be used selectively and documented clearly.\n\n**Example:**\n\nAn AFTER INSERT trigger can write an audit record whenever an employee record changes.\n\nThe application does not need a separate audit INSERT, but the hidden side effect must be understood by maintainers.",
